    "result_text": "All done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing including both new address-structured checks).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe clean insight: everything downstream reads `owner.getAddress()`, so composing the structured fields into `address` during validation makes \"prefer structured, fall back to flat\" transparent to the household hash, postcode validation, locality, and persistence \u2014 no per-reader changes needed.\n\n**Request/response contract** \u2014 `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (generates the DTOs):\n- Added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema, and documented that `address` is the composed value when structured fields are present.\n- Removed `address` from the required list (it's now conditionally required \u2014 either form is accepted), keeping earlier minimal owners valid.\n\n**Validation & composition** \u2014 `ValidateOwnerFields.java` + `OwnerAddress.java`:\n- Normalize whichever address fields are supplied (same trim/collapse/upper-case/street-abbrev rules).\n- When `addressLine1` is non-blank, compose `address` = normalized `addressLine1`, with a single space + normalized `addressLine2` appended only when `addressLine2` is present (new `OwnerAddress.compose` helper); otherwise the flat `address` stands.\n- The existing required-field check on `address` now passes for structured-only owners (composition fills it) and still rejects when neither form supplies an address \u2014 plus `city`.\n\n**Persistence & entity** \u2014 `Owner.java`, `ApplyOwner.java`, and the `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns added to all four DB schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) so the structured fields round-trip and `GET` returns them (`OwnerMapper` maps them by name automatically).\n\nThe tests confirm: a structured `\"10 king st\"` comes back as `addressLine1: \"10 KING STREET\"` and composed `address: \"10 KING STREET\"`, and a flat-address owner is still accepted.\n\nOne note: `JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl` (the inactive `jdbc` profile, not used by tests) still has hand-written column lists that don't include the new columns \u2014 I left it untouched since it's not in scope for the active `spring-data-jpa` profile, but flag it in case you run that profile.",
